---
/* THE HEADER, AND THE PANEL IT OPENS ON A PHONE

   ── WHY THERE IS A MENU AT ALL ─────────────────────────────────────────────

   Above 680px the header carries two ghost links and the action. Below it,
   both ghost links were set to `display:none` -- four items do not fit one row
   and the action is the one that had to survive. That is the correct triage
   and it left the phone with no way to reach any section of a ten-thousand
   pixel page except by scrolling the whole thing.

   So the two links that were being hidden become five in a panel, which is
   also the fix for a second thing: two entries were never the right list. The
   page has six places worth arriving at directly and the header was naming the
   first two because those were the two that fit.

   ── THE PANEL IS A SIBLING OF THE HEADER, NOT A CHILD OF IT ────────────────

   `.top__in` takes a `backdrop-filter` once the page is scrolled, and a filtered
   element becomes the containing block for every `position:fixed` descendant.
   A panel inside it would be fixed to the header rather than to the viewport --
   a 48px-tall full-screen overlay. It is rendered here, beside the header, and
   `.top` never contains it.

   ── IT IS A SCRIPT AND NOT A CHECKBOX ──────────────────────────────────────

   The lane picker in the engines section is four radios and no JavaScript,
   because a radio group is what a one-of-four choice is. This is not that. A
   checkbox toggle cannot close itself when a link inside it is followed, so
   the reader lands on the section with the panel still covering it; it cannot
   lock the scroll behind it, so the page slides under the overlay; and it
   answers no key. All three are what the script below Base.astro's chrome
   block does, and none of them is optional on a surface that covers the page.

   The button is a `<button>` with `aria-expanded` and `aria-controls`, and the
   panel is `hidden` when closed, so nothing inside it is reachable by keyboard
   or by a screen reader while it is shut. */
import Arrow from './Arrow.astro';

const DISCORD = 'https://discord.com/invite/BHfApphz8h';
const SOURCE = 'https://github.com/sw-forge-org/WordScript';

/* THE SIX, IN PAGE ORDER. A menu whose order does not match the order the
   reader would meet these by scrolling is a second, competing structure.

   THE HASHES ARE ROOT-RELATIVE, AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS. This header is in
   `Base.astro`, so it stands on every route -- and the site grew routes the
   day the legal pages landed. A bare `#how` on /imprint is a link to a
   fragment of /imprint, which does not exist: the reader presses it and
   nothing happens at all. `/#how` navigates home and lands on the section from
   anywhere, and on the index it is still the same in-page jump, because Lenis
   resolves a same-document hash whatever the path in front of it. */
const NAV = [
  { href: '/#how', label: 'How it works' },
  { href: '/#turn', label: 'The turn' },
  { href: '/#numbers', label: 'Numbers' },
  { href: '/#what', label: 'What you get' },
  { href: '/#engines', label: 'Engines' },
  { href: '/#faq', label: 'Questions' },
];

/* THE WORDMARK GOES HOME FROM A SUBPAGE AND TO THE TOP FROM THE INDEX.
   One control, two meanings, and the reader's expectation is the same in both:
   take me back to the start of the thing I am reading about. `#top` on
   /imprint would scroll a legal page to a header the reader is already looking
   at. */
const home = Astro.url.pathname === '/';
---
<header class="top" id="top">
  <div class="top__in">
    <a class="brand" href={home ? '#top' : '/'}
       aria-label={home ? 'WordScript, back to top' : 'WordScript, back to the start'}>
      <img src="/assets/logos/wordscript-icon-128.png" width="121" height="128" alt="" />
      <span class="wm" role="img" aria-label="WordScript"></span>
    </a>
    <nav class="top__nav">
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/#how">How it works</a>
      <a class="btn btn--ghost" href="/#numbers">Numbers</a>
      <a class="btn" href={DISCORD}>Join on Discord</a>
      {/* To the RIGHT of the action, because the action is the thing this
          header exists to offer and the menu is how you get past it. */}
      <button class="burger" type="button" id="menu-open"
              aria-controls="menu" aria-expanded="false" aria-label="Open menu"
              data-menu-open>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
        <span aria-hidden="true"></span>
      </button>
    </nav>
  </div>
</header>

<div class="menu" id="menu" hidden tabindex="-1" data-menu>
  {/* The panel repeats the brand rather than letting the header show through.
      A translucent overlay over a page this dense is a page you can half-read,
      and half-readable is worse than covered: the reader's eye keeps trying. */}
  <div class="menu__top">
    <span class="brand">
      <img src="/assets/logos/wordscript-icon-128.png" width="121" height="128" alt="" />
      <span class="wm" role="img" aria-label="WordScript"></span>
    </span>
    <button class="menu__x" type="button" aria-label="Close menu" data-menu-close>
      <svg viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.6"
           stroke-linecap="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M5 5l10 10M15 5L5 15" /></svg>
    </button>
  </div>

  {/* THE ROWS ARE NUMBERED AND THEY DO NOT CARRY AN ARROW.

      A stack of big labels with a chevron on the right is what every phone
      menu looks like, and this one looked like one in particular. The number
      is this page's own way of listing: the demo's three tabs are `01 Cursor`,
      `02 Context`, `03 Agent` in mono at micro size, muted until the one you
      are on. Reused here it is not decoration -- it says the page is read in
      order, which after ADR 0254 pinned the opening is the thing the structure
      is actually claiming. The chevron said only "this is a link", which the
      row already says by being one. */}
  <nav class="menu__list" aria-label="Sections">
    {NAV.map((n, i) => (
      <a href={n.href} data-menu-link>
        <span class="menu__n mono">{String(i + 1).padStart(2, '0')}</span>
        <span class="menu__t">{n.label}</span>
      </a>
    ))}
  </nav>

  {/* THE PAGE'S OWN PAIR, NOT ONE SLAB. The hero and the closing section both
      end on the same two actions, primary then ghost, and a panel that ended
      on a single full-width accent block was inventing a third call to action
      for a page that has one. */}
  <div class="menu__foot">
    <div class="acts">
      <a class="btn btn--primary" href={DISCORD}>Join on Discord<Arrow /></a>
      <a class="btn" href={SOURCE}>Read the source<Arrow /></a>
    </div>
    <a class="menu__mail mono" href="mailto:forge@sw-labs.de">forge@sw-labs.de</a>
  </div>
</div>
